/// United States health savings accounts.
///
/// An HSA is a tax-deferred account for people with a high-deductible health
/// plan. Contributions from pay are untaxed, contributions from a bank account
/// are deducted (`deductible true`, and the money arrives with no basis),
/// growth is untaxed, and money that pays a qualified medical expense comes out
/// untaxed. Anything else that comes out is income, with a 20% tax added before
/// age 65.
///
/// Set `coverage` on each account to `self-only` or `family`: the yearly limit
/// depends on it. Pay medical costs straight from the account into a place of
/// kind `medical`, and the withdrawal is qualified.
///
/// The figures are community-maintained data. Verify them against the IRS
/// (irs.gov, the yearly HSA Rev. Proc.) before relying on them, and correct
/// them in a pull request when they are wrong.
system us/hsa

use us
use std


/// A health savings account (IRC §223).
kind hsa : tax-deferred
  basis zero
  deductible true
  liquidity 3d
  pays medical
  has coverage one of self-only | family

  /// Contributions to an HSA are capped for each person and calendar year,
  /// with a catch-up from age 55 (IRC §223(b)). This deposit took the year over
  /// the cap for the account's `coverage`, counting your employer's
  /// contributions too. Excess contributions are charged 6% for every year they
  /// stay (IRC §4973(g)). The market's growth is not a contribution.
  ///
  /// To fix: ask payroll to lower the contribution, and withdraw the excess
  /// before the tax filing deadline.
  law contribution-limit
    on in
    when not from is market | tax-deferred
    when not purpose is #interest-income | #dividend | #gain
    count amount as hsa-contributions
    let extra = if owner.age >= 55y then catch-up[year] else empty
    require value(tally(hsa-contributions), USD) <= limit[year, self.coverage] + extra "HSA contributions over the yearly limit"

  /// A withdrawal that did not pay a qualified medical expense owes an
  /// additional 20% of what is taxable, unless the owner is 65 or older
  /// (IRC §223(f)(4)). This withdrawal went somewhere other than a place of kind
  /// `medical`, so it is priced at that 20%.
  ///
  /// To fix: to reimburse yourself for an expense the account should have paid,
  /// which the IRS allows with your receipts, or when an exception applies
  /// (death or disability), keep the withdrawal and mark it `!` with the
  /// reason.
  law nonqualified-hsa-penalty
    on gain
    when gain > empty
    when not to is market
    require (purpose is #medical or to is tax-deferred) or owner.age >= 65y else owe 20% * gain to irs by date(year + 1, 4, 15) as nonqualified-hsa-penalty "HSA withdrawal that did not pay a medical expense"


// The contribution limit by coverage (IRC §223(b)(2)).
// Rev. Proc. 2023-23 (2024), Rev. Proc. 2024-25 (2025), Rev. Proc. 2025-19 (2026).
param limit
  2024 self-only 4_150 USD
  2024 family    8_300 USD
  2025 self-only 4_300 USD
  2025 family    8_550 USD
  2026 self-only 4_400 USD
  2026 family    8_750 USD

// The catch-up for age 55 and over (IRC §223(b)(3)). It is not indexed.
param catch-up
  2024 1_000 USD
